Step 2: Analyzing the Cipher

The code implements AES-128 but with critical modifications:

    sub_bytes() does nothing - returns the state unchanged

    sub_word() does nothing - used in key schedule

    rcon() does nothing - also used in key schedule

This means:

    No S-box = No non-linear substitution

    The cipher becomes linear (actually affine) over GF(2)

    Encryption can be written as: c = L(p) ⊕ K where:

        L is a linear transformation (ShiftRows + MixColumns)

        K is a constant derived from all round keys

Step 3: The Linear Property

For any two plaintexts p1 and p2:
text

c1 ⊕ c2 = [L(p1) ⊕ K] ⊕ [L(p2) ⊕ K] = L(p1) ⊕ L(p2) = L(p1 ⊕ p2)

The K cancels out! This is the key insight.
